Cool to see this pop up today.I'm building a new language and just a couple of days ago the concept of guard methods came up as I was trying to tighten up equality semantics to be more like Swift.Things like Array.contains() only work if the element type implements the Equatable interface, so it would be a guard method. Maybe something like:    class Array<T> {
      contains(value: T): boolean where T extends Equatable { ... }
    }

Or possibly a constraint on the `this` type, TypeScript style:    class Array<T> {
      contains(this: Array<T extends Equatable>, value: T): boolean { ... }
    }

